# Git & CI/CD Standards

## Commit Message Format (Conventional Commits)
```
<type>(<scope>): <short description>

[optional body — explain WHY, not WHAT]

[optional footer: BREAKING CHANGE or issue refs]
```

Types: `feat` · `fix` · `chore` · `refactor` · `test` · `docs` · `perf` · `ci`  
Scope examples: `leads` · `auth` · `locker` · `ui` · `api`

Examples:
```
feat(leads): add CSV bulk import with preview table
fix(auth): queue concurrent requests during token refresh
perf(deals): virtualise pipeline kanban for large datasets
test(locker): achieve 100% coverage on encrypt/decrypt utils
```

## Branch Naming
- `feat/<ticket>-short-description`
- `fix/<ticket>-short-description`
- `chore/<description>`

## PR Rules
- Every PR must pass: `lint → typecheck → test:ci → build`
- Lighthouse CI budget must not regress
- No PR merged with TypeScript errors or ESLint warnings
- Test coverage must not drop below thresholds

## CI Pipeline Steps
1. `npm ci`
2. `npm run lint` (ESLint + TypeScript check)
3. `npm run test:ci` (Vitest --coverage)
4. `npm run build` (Vite production build)
5. `npx lighthouse-ci` (Core Web Vitals budget)
6. Docker build + push (on main only)
7. Deploy to staging → smoke test → promote to prod

## Package Management
- Use `npm` (matches `package-lock.json` in repo)
- Never commit `node_modules`
- Run `npm audit` before merging — block on high/critical severity
